1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,430
Welcome to School Computer Science,
Explained. This is AP Computer Science

2
00:00:05,430 --> 00:00:10,932
Principles, Big Idea 3, part two. Your
programs will make decisions, repeat

3
00:00:10,932 --> 00:00:12,693
steps, and change lists.

4
00:00:13,867 --> 00:00:17,707
A program that always does the same thing
isn't very smart.

5
00:00:18,157 --> 00:00:19,736
Conditionals let it choose.

6
00:00:20,886 --> 00:00:26,865
In AP pseudocode, IF is followed by a
condition in parentheses, and a block in

7
00:00:26,865 --> 00:00:27,862
curly braces.

8
00:00:28,312 --> 00:00:33,932
IF, open paren, score greater than or
equal to sixty, close paren. Then in the

9
00:00:33,932 --> 00:00:35,373
block, DISPLAY pass.

10
00:00:36,523 --> 00:00:41,709
If the condition is true, the block runs.
If it's false, the block is skipped, and

11
00:00:41,709 --> 00:00:43,670
the program continues after it.

12
00:00:44,820 --> 00:00:46,398
Add ELSE for a second path.

13
00:00:46,848 --> 00:00:52,888
IF score is at least sixty, DISPLAY pass.
ELSE, DISPLAY try again. Exactly one of

14
00:00:52,888 --> 00:00:54,379
the two blocks runs.

15
00:00:55,529 --> 00:00:59,241
On the exam, you'll often trace code with
a specific value.

16
00:00:59,691 --> 00:01:04,904
With a score of fifty five, the condition
is false, so the ELSE block runs, and it

17
00:01:04,904 --> 00:01:06,112
displays try again.

18
00:01:07,262 --> 00:01:12,899
In block-based code, the same idea appears
as an if block shaped like a C, with the

19
00:01:12,899 --> 00:01:14,665
condition in the top slot.

20
00:01:15,115 --> 00:01:16,992
The meaning is exactly the same.

21
00:01:18,142 --> 00:01:24,795
Your turn. x gets four. IF x MOD two
equals zero, DISPLAY even. ELSE, DISPLAY

22
00:01:24,795 --> 00:01:26,782
odd. What is displayed?

23
00:01:27,232 --> 00:01:28,512
Pause the video now.

24
00:01:28,962 --> 00:01:33,869
Four MOD two is zero, so the condition is
true. It displays even.

25
00:01:34,319 --> 00:01:37,220
Next, we'll put conditionals inside
conditionals.

26
00:01:38,370 --> 00:01:40,759
Some decisions have more than two
outcomes.

27
00:01:41,209 --> 00:01:46,116
For those, we put a conditional inside
another one. That's a nested conditional.

28
00:01:47,266 --> 00:01:52,916
Here's a movie ticket price. IF age is
less than thirteen, price gets eight.

29
00:01:52,916 --> 00:01:58,863
ELSE, inside it, IF age is sixty five or
more, price gets nine. ELSE, price gets

30
00:01:58,863 --> 00:01:59,383
twelve.

31
00:02:00,533 --> 00:02:05,781
Trace it with age seventy. Is seventy less
than thirteen? No, so go to the ELSE.

32
00:02:06,231 --> 00:02:09,581
Is it at least sixty five? Yes. So the
price is nine.

33
00:02:10,731 --> 00:02:14,528
The inner IF is only checked when the
outer condition is false.

34
00:02:14,978 --> 00:02:20,499
With age ten, the first condition is true,
the price is eight, and the inner IF never

35
00:02:20,499 --> 00:02:20,823
runs.

36
00:02:21,973 --> 00:02:27,082
Nested conditionals can often be rewritten
with AND or OR, and they'll do the same

37
00:02:27,082 --> 00:02:27,456
thing.

38
00:02:27,906 --> 00:02:33,359
On the exam, a common question asks which
two code segments always give the same

39
00:02:33,359 --> 00:02:33,837
result.

40
00:02:34,987 --> 00:02:40,136
To check, try values from every case,
including the boundaries, like exactly

41
00:02:40,136 --> 00:02:42,304
thirteen and exactly sixty five.

42
00:02:43,454 --> 00:02:48,728
Your turn. Using the ticket code, what is
the price for age sixty five, and for age

43
00:02:48,728 --> 00:02:49,299
thirteen?

44
00:02:49,749 --> 00:02:51,029
Pause the video now.

45
00:02:51,479 --> 00:02:57,467
Sixty five is not under thirteen, but it
is sixty five or more, so nine. Thirteen

46
00:02:57,467 --> 00:02:59,906
fails both conditions, so twelve.

47
00:03:00,356 --> 00:03:02,596
Next, we'll repeat code with iteration.

48
00:03:03,746 --> 00:03:08,610
Iteration means repeating steps. The AP
exam has two kinds of loops.

49
00:03:09,060 --> 00:03:11,215
REPEAT n TIMES, and REPEAT UNTIL.

50
00:03:12,365 --> 00:03:15,842
REPEAT n TIMES runs its block exactly n
times.

51
00:03:16,292 --> 00:03:22,514
x gets one. REPEAT three TIMES, x gets x
times two. x becomes two, then four, then

52
00:03:22,514 --> 00:03:22,969
eight.

53
00:03:24,119 --> 00:03:29,680
REPEAT UNTIL runs while its condition is
false, and stops as soon as the condition

54
00:03:29,680 --> 00:03:30,562
becomes true.

55
00:03:31,012 --> 00:03:33,743
The condition is checked before each
repetition.

56
00:03:34,893 --> 00:03:39,885
n gets one. REPEAT UNTIL n is greater than
ten, n gets n times three.

57
00:03:40,335 --> 00:03:45,905
n becomes three, then nine, then twenty
seven. Now n is greater than ten, so the

58
00:03:45,905 --> 00:03:46,671
loop stops.

59
00:03:47,821 --> 00:03:52,514
If the condition is already true at the
start, the block runs zero times.

60
00:03:52,964 --> 00:03:56,825
And if the condition never becomes true,
you get an infinite loop.

61
00:03:57,975 --> 00:04:03,018
Use REPEAT n TIMES when you know the
count. Use REPEAT UNTIL when you repeat

62
00:04:03,018 --> 00:04:04,610
until something happens.

63
00:04:05,760 --> 00:04:11,450
Your turn. count gets zero. REPEAT UNTIL
count equals five, count gets count plus

64
00:04:11,450 --> 00:04:12,715
two. What happens?

65
00:04:13,165 --> 00:04:14,445
Pause the video now.

66
00:04:14,895 --> 00:04:20,312
count goes zero, two, four, six, eight,
and never equals five. It's an infinite

67
00:04:20,312 --> 00:04:20,655
loop.

68
00:04:21,105 --> 00:04:23,003
Next, we'll develop algorithms.

69
00:04:24,153 --> 00:04:27,481
An algorithm is a set of steps to solve a
problem.

70
00:04:27,931 --> 00:04:32,624
Programmers build new algorithms by
combining a few familiar patterns.

71
00:04:33,774 --> 00:04:38,671
Every algorithm can be built from three
parts: sequencing, selection, and

72
00:04:38,671 --> 00:04:39,342
iteration.

73
00:04:39,792 --> 00:04:43,803
Steps in order, decisions with IF, and
repetition with loops.

74
00:04:44,953 --> 00:04:50,717
Some patterns come up again and again.
Finding the largest or smallest value.

75
00:04:50,717 --> 00:04:56,032
Computing a sum or an average. And
checking if a number is divisible by

76
00:04:56,032 --> 00:04:57,454
another, using MOD.

77
00:04:58,604 --> 00:05:04,373
Here's finding the maximum. max gets the
first item. Then for each item, IF the

78
00:05:04,373 --> 00:05:07,586
item is greater than max, max gets the
item.

79
00:05:08,036 --> 00:05:10,638
At the end, max holds the largest value.

80
00:05:11,788 --> 00:05:14,647
Different algorithms can solve the same
problem.

81
00:05:15,097 --> 00:05:20,345
And the same algorithm can be written in
different ways, in pseudocode, in blocks,

82
00:05:20,345 --> 00:05:22,009
or in different languages.

83
00:05:23,159 --> 00:05:28,553
When you build an algorithm, test it with
normal values and with edge cases, like a

84
00:05:28,553 --> 00:05:31,543
list where the largest value is first or
last.

85
00:05:32,693 --> 00:05:37,728
Your turn. To check if n is divisible by
three, what condition should you use?

86
00:05:38,178 --> 00:05:39,458
Pause the video now.

87
00:05:39,908 --> 00:05:46,116
n MOD three equals zero. If the remainder
is zero, three divides n evenly.

88
00:05:46,566 --> 00:05:49,382
Next, we'll change lists with list
operations.

89
00:05:50,532 --> 00:05:53,220
Lists can grow and shrink while a program
runs.

90
00:05:53,670 --> 00:05:58,001
The AP exam reference sheet gives four
list operations to know.

91
00:05:59,151 --> 00:06:03,055
APPEND, list comma value, adds the value
to the end of the list.

92
00:06:03,505 --> 00:06:08,433
If nums is five, eight, then APPEND nums
comma two makes it five, eight, two.

93
00:06:09,583 --> 00:06:15,389
INSERT, list comma i comma value, puts the
value at index i, and shifts the later

94
00:06:15,389 --> 00:06:16,751
items to the right.

95
00:06:17,201 --> 00:06:21,467
INSERT nums comma one comma nine makes it
nine, five, eight, two.

96
00:06:22,617 --> 00:06:28,633
REMOVE, list comma i, removes the item at
index i, and shifts the later items left.

97
00:06:29,083 --> 00:06:33,265
REMOVE nums comma two removes the five,
leaving nine, eight, two.

98
00:06:34,415 --> 00:06:39,880
LENGTH of list gives the number of items.
After every change, the length changes

99
00:06:39,880 --> 00:06:40,153
too.

100
00:06:40,603 --> 00:06:44,379
Remember, indexes start at one, and the
last index is LENGTH.

101
00:06:45,529 --> 00:06:50,918
You can also assign directly, list bracket
i gets value, which replaces one item

102
00:06:50,918 --> 00:06:52,804
without changing the length.

103
00:06:53,954 --> 00:06:59,527
Your turn. a gets three, six. APPEND a
comma nine. INSERT a comma two comma four.

104
00:06:59,527 --> 00:07:01,591
REMOVE a comma one. What is a?

105
00:07:02,041 --> 00:07:03,321
Pause the video now.

106
00:07:03,771 --> 00:07:09,697
After APPEND: three, six, nine. After
INSERT at two: three, four, six, nine.

107
00:07:09,697 --> 00:07:12,582
After REMOVE at one: four, six, nine.

108
00:07:13,032 --> 00:07:16,168
Next, we'll loop through lists with FOR
EACH, and search them.

109
00:07:17,318 --> 00:07:21,995
If this helped, subscribe to School
Computer Science, Explained, so you don't

110
00:07:21,995 --> 00:07:23,270
miss the next lesson.

111
00:07:23,720 --> 00:07:24,616
See you there.

